{"records":[{"id":"ddmkcddn5c","title":"TFF2 Interacts with A4GNT to Regulate Inflammation to Protect against Pneumonia during Influenza Virus Infection","description":"Trefoil factor 2 (TFF2) has been shown to reduce inflammation and promote mucosal repair in models of gastric and colonic injury. However, the role of TFF2 in acute respiratory tract virus infection remains elusive. Here, we demonstrate that TFF2 protects mice against pneumonia in influenza virus infections. In vitro studies have revealed that TFF2 recognizes the terminal GlcNAc-α-1,4-Gal disaccharide of cell surface proteins mediated by the glycosyltransferase activity of α1,4-N-acetylglucosaminyltransferase (A4GNT). Functionally, TFF2 organized membranous TFF2-A4GNT-glycan protein complex serves to restrain cellular inflammation pathways by augmenting inhibitory Tyr527 phosphorylation at the C-terminus of src-family kinases (SFKs), thereby effectively preventing the phosphorylation of stimulatory SFKs Tyr416. Finally, we have conclusively verified that the protective effect of TFF2 treatment relies on the TFF2-A4GNT-glycan axis during influenza virus infection. In the future, TFF2 may offer a new potential intervention strategy for inflammatory diseases induced by acute respiratory infections.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-25T18:37:11.714Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ddmkcddn5c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ddmkcddn5c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weihui","last_name":"Fu","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82101846"},{"id":"https://ror.org/05pwfyy15","name":"National Centre for Research and Development","grant_id":"2022YFC2604100"}],"categories":["Immunology","Infectious Disease","Influenza Virus","Acute Inflammation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3xw35xczs","title":"Disrupting the HDAC6-ubiquitin interaction impairs infection by influenza and Zika virus and cellular stress pathways","description":"The original data for manuscript: \"Disrupting the HDAC6-ubiquitin interaction impairs infection by influenza and Zika virus and cellular stress pathways\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-18T12:11:32.362Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3xw35xczs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3xw35xczs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Longlong","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rwxp2nfvys","title":"The immunological basis for antibody responsiveness following influenza virus infection.","description":"This dataset is based on a study to identify cellular immunologic markers associated with robust antibody responses or seroconversion after influenza virus infection. The dataset were derived from two independent human cohorts, SHIVERS and FLU09, based in Auckland, New Zealand and Memphis, Tennessee, USA, respectively. SHIVERS dataset contain demogrphic, serology and flow cytometric data of PBMCs collected from confirmed influenza individuals. FLU09 dataset contain demographic, influenza specific IgA and IgG and flow cytometric data of cells collected from nasal washings of confirmed influenza individuals.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-13T13:26:42.778Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rwxp2nfvys","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rwxp2nfvys"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sook San","last_name":"Wong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Clinical Immunology","Antibody","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pjdhv7wkm8","title":"Influenza A virus infection is exacerbated by CXCL10+ inflammatory eosinophils through enhanced CD8⁺ T-cell-mediated immunopathology","description":" Data set for 《Influenza A virus infection is exacerbated by CXCL10+ inflammatory eosinophils through enhanced CD8⁺ T-cell-mediated immunopathology》","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-19T15:29:35.468Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pjdhv7wkm8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pjdhv7wkm8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yingying","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wxk9gnw22r","title":"Quantitative temporal proteomic analysis of vaccinia virus infection reveals regulation of histone deacetylases by an interferon antagonist","description":"Vaccinia virus (VACV) has numerous immune evasion strategies, including multiple mechanisms of inhibition of IRF-3, NF-κB and type I interferon (IFN) signaling. Here, we use highly multiplexed proteomics to quantify ~9,000 cellular proteins and ~80% of viral proteins at seven time points throughout VACV infection. 265 cellular proteins are downregulated >2 fold by VACV, including putative natural killer cell ligands and IFN-stimulated genes. Two thirds of these viral targets, including the class II histone deacetylase HDAC5, are degraded proteolytically during infection. In follow-up analysis, we demonstrate that HDAC5 restricts replication of both VACV and herpes simplex virus type 1. By generating a protein-based temporal classification of VACV gene expression, we identify protein C6, a multifunctional IFN antagonist, as being necessary and sufficient for proteasomal degradation of HDAC5. Our approach thus identifies both a host antiviral factor and a viral mechanism of innate immune evasion.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-05-07T17:03:10.299Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wxk9gnw22r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wxk9gnw22r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Weekes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kccn9d4htz","title":"Dataset on effect of thymol against influenza A virus in A549 cells","description":"Influenza A virus, enveloped, segmented, negative strand RNA virus belongs to the family of Orthomyxoviridae, a major respiratory pathogen and responsible for causing number of global and local outbreaks. Currently, there are three classes of anti-influenza drugs with different antiviral mechanisms, including M2 ion channel blockers (amantadine and rimantadine), viral RNA synthesis inhibitors. However, evolution of NA inhibitor-resistant influenza viruses have also emerged, thus limiting the future utility of NA inhibitors. Thus the objective was to explore a low-toxic anti-influenza drug. \nThe cytotoxicity assay of thymol was performed by MTT assay. Varying concentration of thymol ranging from 100 µg/ml to 0.39 µg/ml was added to A549 cells and absorbance was recorded at 570nm after 24hr. The data obtained was expressed in percentages. Based on the data, IC50 obtained was 49.5 µg/ml. Hence, concentrations from 50 µg/ml to 6.25 µg/ml was used for all the experiments. Thymol was evaluated against influenza A virus by real time PCR . RNA was isolated post infection and treatment and relative expression was measured by qPCR. The Ct value obtained was normalized against GAPDH. The real time PCR showed significant down regulation of IAV at 50 and 25 µg/ml. To further validate the anti-viral efficacy, the cells were further subjected to western blotting after treatment with thymol and infection with influenza A virus. Cellular protein was isolated and separated using 12 % SDS PAGE. The blot was probed using anti-PB1 influenza antibody. The expression was detected using chemiluminescence detection kit and chemidoc imaging system. The band intensity was quantified and plotted on a graph to measure the difference in expression of PB1 in both thymol treated and uninfected cells. The western blotting results showed significant inhibition of viral protein PB1 at 50 and 25 µg/ml, which corroborates with the real time PCR data. Thus, confirming the anti-viral efficacy of thymol against influenza A virus PR8 at 24hr in A549 cells. The graphs were statistically analyzed using One-Way analysis of variance (ANOVA). \nThe data obtained might be useful in initiating in-vivo studies for establishing thymol as an effective treatment option against influenza A virus.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-29T14:29:00.684Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kccn9d4htz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kccn9d4htz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tanusri","last_name":"Nandi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Madhu","last_name":"Khanna","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xykyffh2xv","title":"Host Transcriptional Corepressor TLE1 is commonly exploited by RNA viruses for replication","description":"This dataset delineates alterations in host genes transcriptionally regulated by Transducin-like enhancer of split 1 (TLE1) following influenza virus infection. TLE1, a transcriptional co-repressor encoded at chromosome 9q21.32, is clinically utilized as a diagnostic marker for synovial sarcoma. However, our analysis reveals that TLE1-regulated gene sets identified by ChIP enrichment are distinct post-infection, indicating its previously uncharacterized role in modulating transcriptional reprogramming during viral challenge. These findings provide novel insights for identifying host-directed antiviral targets and elucidate mechanisms by which influenza virus subverts host gene expression to facilitate replication and pathogenesis, thereby highlighting potential therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-17T13:54:13.077Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xykyffh2xv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xykyffh2xv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaomeng","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haoran","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peng","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"kailai","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuofeng","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Influenza Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kfhtngkjrj","title":"Layered Protein Nanoparticles Containing Influenza B HA stalk Inducted Sustained Cross-Protection against Viruses Spanning Both Viral Lineages","description":"In this study, we generated structure-stabilized HA stalk antigens from influenza B and fabricated double-layered protein nanoparticles as universal influenza B vaccine candidates. In vitro studies found that the resulting protein nanoparticles were effectively taken up to activate dendritic cells. Nanoparticle immunization induced broadly reactive immune responses conferring robust and sustained cross-immune protection against influenza B virus strains of both lineages. The results reveal the potential of layered protein nanoparticles incorporated with structure-stabilized constant antigens as a universal influenza vaccine with improved immune protective potency and breadth.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-27T16:09:47.717Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kfhtngkjrj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kfhtngkjrj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Wandi","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Nanoparticles","Influenza Vaccine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9pw3yyyr4c","title":"AgeingDysbiosis_GutLungAxis_Influenza_rnaseq_metagenomics","description":"Data derived from gene expression analysis: \"Age-Associated Gut Microbiota Dysbiosis Impairs Antiviral Defense During Influenza Virus Infection and Identifies Acetate as a Protective Mediator in Senescent Human Lung Cells\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-02T18:13:19.497Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9pw3yyyr4c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9pw3yyyr4c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Christoph","last_name":"Wernike","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stefanie","last_name":"Deinhardt-Emmer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metagenomics","RNA Sequencing","Aging","Influenza Virus","Gut Microbiome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ytvttnr2nn","title":"Code for SPS calculations","description":"Codes to calculate spatial proximity scores (SPSs) between multiplexed cell units (MCUs), used in the paper \"Cellular state landscape and herpes simplex virus type 1 infection progression are connected\" by Pietilä et al., 2023.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-24T15:17:03.19Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ytvttnr2nn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ytvttnr2nn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maija","last_name":"Pietilä","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Janne","last_name":"Ravantti","orcid":"0000-0002-8578-7230"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Multiplexing","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":44863,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":63,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":44863,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":44780,"restricted":30,"embargoed":53},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":850},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":348},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":217},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":189},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":174},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":173},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":170},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":149},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":135},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":97}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":43768},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":744},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":111},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":48},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":43},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":34},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":25},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":22},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":16},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":15}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":202},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":202},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":191},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":111},{"id":"a2ddc67d-42e6-5cdb-a9ea-4515837fc4c4","name":"Universitas Airlangga","count":111}]}}